      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© 2014 European Pain Federation - EFIC®",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND",
            "content": "For effective treatment of acute pain, a rapid onset of action is important. Here we quantify the antinociceptive profile of an orodispersible oxycodone tablet (OOT) in a randomized, double-blind, active comparator (paracetamol orodispersible tablet, POT), crossover study design in a population of healthy volunteers."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "Twelve female volunteers were randomized to receive 20\u2009mg OOT and 500\u2009mg POT sublingually on two occasions. The electrical pain threshold (EPTh), electrical pain tolerance (EPTol) and pressure pain threshold (PPT) were obtained at regular intervals for 5\u2009h. Time-response data were analysed with a longitudinal pharmacodynamic model characterized by rate constants for analgesia onset (kON ), offset (kOFF ), potency parameter (EFF) and validated with a bootstrap analysis. Values are the median (95% CI) as derived from the bootstrap analysis."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "OOT produced a rapid increase in response values. For electrical pain analgesia onset, t½kON , 44 (25-67) versus analgesia offset, t½kOFF , 156 (63-552) min, p\u2009<\u20090.01. For pressure pain, t½kON equalled t½kOFF : 30 (16-48)\u2009min. OOT was most potent on EPTol: EFF 0.95 (0.39-1.71), p\u2009<\u20090.01, with similar potencies on EPTh, 0.43 (0.19-0.87) and PPT, 0.40 (0.21-0.67). Paracetamol displayed 14% of the analgesic efficacy of oxycodone."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "The analgesic effect of orodispersible oxycodone was successfully quantified using a mathematical model of analgesia evolution. This method allows quantification of a variety of responses times from sparse data sets. Response times as defined by a 30% increase in response thresholds varied significantly among end points: EPTol 15\u2009min, PPTh 18\u2009min and EPTh 41\u2009min."
          }
        ]
      },
